  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records collectively establish that thermal treatment of high-entropy alloys can produce element-dependent diffusion, segregation, phase instability, surface depletion, and selective evaporation rather than universally sluggish kinetics. R7 links rapid heating to preferential evaporation, while R11 and R15 show fast outward diffusion and depletion of a particular constituent; R3 also identifies surface free energy as a relevant lever. However, none describes in-situ vacuum heating of sub-10 nm AuCoCuNiPt nanoparticles or predicts that surface-energy differences specifically drive Au and Cu surface segregation, with particle reshaping enhancing diffusion and causing nanoparticle disappearance and size-distribution changes. Thus the literature points in the right general direction, but combining these ideas into the later intervention and nanoparticle-specific mechanism would require a substantial new step."
